<Base lang="en" page="start" title="Getting started · Traces Blanches" description="Install the app (planned), build a first offline pack with the project's command line, and contribute to the Traces Blanches code.">
<div class="text">
<h1>Getting started</h1>
<p class="lead">Three paths, depending on what you want to do: install the app, build a pack on your computer, or contribute to the code.</p>
<ul class="toc">
<li><a href="#install">Install the app</a></li>
<li><a href="#pack">Build a first pack</a></li>
<li><a href="#contribute">Contribute to the code</a></li>
</ul>

<h2 id="install">Install the app</h2>
<p>The app is not available yet. It will come out on Google Play first, as an internal test, then for everyone. The iPhone version will follow.</p>
<p>To know when it comes out, follow <a href="https://github.com/tracesblanches/tracesblanches/issues/122">the Google Play release issue</a> on GitHub.</p>

<h2 id="pack">Build a first pack</h2>
<p>Today, a pack is built on a computer with the project's command line. You then look at it in the viewer, in your browser. The maps on this site come from there.</p>

<h3>What you need</h3>
<ul>
<li>A Linux computer. The project is developed and tested on Linux.</li>
<li><code>git</code> and <a href="https://rustup.rs/">rustup</a>. The repository pins the Rust version in <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code>; <code>rustup toolchain install</code>, run inside the repository, installs it.</li>
<li>An internet connection for the first run, and a few gigabytes of disk. The source cache takes 5 GB for the two test sites.</li>
<li>Optional: a Météo-France API key, for the day's avalanche bulletin. Without a key, the data pipeline reads the public archive of bulletins.</li>
</ul>

<h3>1. Get the code</h3>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/tracesblanches/tracesblanches
cd tracesblanches
cargo run --release -p skimap-cli -- sites</code></pre>
<p>The last command builds the command line, then lists the known sites. The command line prints in French:</p>
<pre><code>cervieres          Cervières (05, INSEE 05027)
  massif Météo-France   : 17 Queyras (vérifié sur les polygones snowtools) ; BERA : oui
chapelle-des-bois  Chapelle-des-Bois (25, INSEE 25121)
  massif Météo-France   : 55 Haut-Doubs / 56 Haut-Jura (vérifié sur les polygones snowtools) ; BERA : non</code></pre>

<h3>2. Build the pack</h3>
<pre><code>cargo run --release -p skimap-cli -- build-all cervieres</code></pre>
<p>The command downloads the sources, computes each layer in the order of their dependencies, then writes the pack to <code>data/out/cervieres/pack/</code>. It prints one line per step, with its time and key figures:</p>
<pre><code>build-all cervieres (Cervières)
planet                24.05s  33113 pistes dans la zone France, données OSM du 2026-11-13T23:00:00Z
pistes                 0.14s  195 ways, 66.72 km dont 61.56 km nordiques ; graphe 189 nœuds, 224 arêtes (1 connecteur …
…
terrain               10.80s  324 dalles, 3317 x 3480 cellules, 87.5 % valides, pente ≥ 30° : 39.6 % (rapport 07 : 38 …
snow                   2.81s  simulation du 2026-11-14, 405034 cellules estimées, hauteur moyenne 7.6 cm (min 0, max …
…
avalanche-exposure   324.36s  3317 x 3480 cellules de 5 m ; terrain peu exposé (modèle) 9.3 %, exposé 51.8 %, très ex …
…
coverage               0.93s  ARCEP 2026_T2 : partie française 225.59 km², sans aucun réseau 51.25 km² (22.72 %), 117 …
bera                   0.01s  Unavailable, massif Queyras, origine -, risque max -
meteo                      -  ignoré : personal_sources désactivé (SKIMAP_PERSONAL_SOURCES)
…
build-pack             4.11s  pack 20261115T074709Z : 39.61 Mo, 34 couches, 4 absentes ; relecture : OK
…
total                386.97s</code></pre>
<p>The first run downloads the sources: LiDAR HD terrain, Plan IGN tiles, an OpenStreetMap extract, the snow simulation, the ARCEP maps. With the cache already filled, the build takes a few minutes, most of them for avalanche exposure. The Cervières pack then weighs 39.6 MB, the Chapelle-des-Bois pack 35.0 MB.</p>
<p>Sources restricted to personal use stay disabled by default: they never go into a published pack. Their steps are then skipped.</p>

<h3>3. Look at the pack</h3>
<pre><code>cargo run --release -p skimap-cli -- serve</code></pre>
<p>Then open <code>http://127.0.0.1:8765/tools/viewer/index.html?pack=../../data/out/cervieres/pack/</code> in your browser. The viewer's panel picks the map style (cross-country, backcountry, guidance) and the layers; tapping a feature opens its details. The viewer loads MapLibre and PMTiles from jsDelivr the first time; after that, all data comes from the pack, on your machine.</p>

<h3>4. Compute a route</h3>
<pre><code>cargo run --release -p skimap-cli -- route cervieres \
  44.85894,6.725006 44.84465,6.8157 --date 2027-01-15 --start 13:00</code></pre>
<pre><code>44.85894,6.72501 → 44.84465,6.81570 (cervieres, style classique) : 11.36 km, D+ 462 m, D− 145 m
temps estimé (C5) : 2h15 (montée 1h48, plat 0h22, descente 0h05), 5.1 km/h de moyenne
C7, départ 13:00 le 2027-01-15 : coucher du soleil 17:16, fin du crépuscule civil 17:49, retour au plus tard 17:19 (crépuscule − 30 min) ; arrivée prévue 15:41 (temps × 1.2), marge 1h38</code></pre>
<p>The route is written as GeoJSON to <code>route.geojson</code>. The other commands are described in <a href="https://github.com/tracesblanches/tracesblanches/blob/main/docs/architecture/compiler.md" hreflang="fr">docs/architecture/compiler.md</a> (in French).</p>

<h2 id="contribute">Contribute to the code</h2>
<p>The repository is a Rust workspace: one crate per domain (trails, terrain, snow, zones, huts, network, avalanches, pack, core, voice) and a command line that ties them together. <a href="https://github.com/tracesblanches/tracesblanches/blob/main/docs/architecture.md" hreflang="fr">docs/architecture.md</a> (in French) maps it out. The Android app, in Kotlin, will join it.</p>
<ol>
<li>Pick an issue, or open one to describe your change before you spend time on it.</li>
<li>Write the test that proves the change. If a reference output moves, update it in the same commit and explain the difference.</li>
<li>Run the checks:
<pre><code>cargo fmt --all --check
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo test --workspace</code></pre></li>
<li>Sign off each commit with <code>git commit -s</code>, under the Developer Certificate of Origin.</li>
<li>Open the pull request. Paste the output of the commands and tick the sentence agreeing to the licence and to the app store exception.</li>
</ol>
<p>Contributions written with an AI tool are welcome: you review them, you bring the evidence and you answer for them as for your own code.</p>
